{"prompt": "A square, purely nonrepresentational abstract painting that fills the entire canvas edge to edge, with no frame, border, wall, room, or gallery setting. It is oil and cold wax on heavy canvas, built from many layers. The mood is sustained pressure held in check by deliberate restraint.

Palette: a deep ink-blue and blackened teal ground, scraped thin in places so a dull bone-white undercoat glows through. Against it, a few harsh, narrow passages of hot vermilion and burnt orange. Small flashes of acid yellow-green appear only at the sharpest points of contrast. A broad band of cool pale grey-white sits slightly off-center and is left mostly empty, so the eye can rest there.

Composition: the left two-thirds is dense and turbulent. Short, fast, overlapping palette-knife strokes and dry-brush drags pile up in competing directions, with thick impasto ridges and scumbled, semi-opaque veils over them. Some strokes are dragged to a ragged stop. Others are wiped out and overpainted, leaving ghosted edges and visible corrections. Toward the right the gestures slow into long, level, patient sweeps of translucent blue-grey glaze, with fine scratched lines through the wet paint. A single heavy vermilion slash cuts across the dense zone and is partly buried under cooler layers, as if resisted. The tension between the crowded, urgent field and the quiet open field drives the whole image. There is a steady rhythm of staccato marks against slow, breathing sweeps. Rough, gritty surface texture alternates with smooth, glassy patches. The lighting is even and painterly, flat to the canvas, with no depth illusion beyond layered paint. No recognizable objects, figures, faces, bodies, scenes, landscapes, horizons, machines, diagrams, symbols, icons, letters, numbers, or text."}

